# Parse at the Boundary — One Source of Truth

Raw external data (wire strings, JSON, PlayerPrefs, config text) is converted to typed domain values **once**, at the ingestion boundary. Consumers receive enums and typed models — they never re-parse strings or re-derive values. Fallback/default decisions for invalid data are made at that same boundary, in one place.

## The two failure modes this prevents

1. **Late parsing.** A wire string (`"character_model"`, `"kind"`) flows deep into consuming code, and every use site does its own `TryParse` + fallback. The parse logic and the fallback decision get duplicated, drift apart, and fail differently in different screens.
2. **Duplicated state.** The raw DTO list is stored *and* per-entity flags live in parallel collections (`HashSet<string> readyIds` next to the roster list, `Dictionary<string,int> counts` keyed by the same ids). Every mutation must remember to touch all of them; removal forgets one and leaves stale state behind.

## BAD — consumer re-parses, state lives in three places

```csharp
class GameState
{
    public List<UnitDto> units;                    // raw wire DTOs stored
    public HashSet<string> stunnedIds = new();     // parallel map #1
    public Dictionary<string, int> hp = new();     // parallel map #2
}

void SpawnUnit(UnitDto dto)  // deep in presentation
{
    if (!Enum.TryParse(dto.Kind, out UnitKind kind))
    {
        kind = UnitKind.Grunt; // local fallback, duplicated at every use site
    }
    bool stunned = state.stunnedIds.Contains(dto.Id); // hope it was kept in sync
}
```

## GOOD — one typed model, converted once

```csharp
sealed class Unit
{
    public string Id;
    public UnitKind Kind;   // parsed once at ingestion; fallback decided there
    public bool IsStunned;  // folded in — no parallel set
    public int Hp;
}

// The ONLY place UnitDto becomes Unit; raw DTO is discarded after conversion.
Unit Ingest(UnitDto dto)
{
    if (!Enum.TryParse(dto.Kind, out UnitKind kind))
    {
        Debug.LogError($"[GameState] Invalid kind '{dto.Kind}' for unit '{dto.Id}'; falling back to Grunt.");
        kind = UnitKind.Grunt;
    }
    return new Unit { Id = dto.Id, Kind = kind, Hp = dto.Hp };
}

void SpawnUnit(Unit unit) => Spawn(unit.Kind); // no TryParse anywhere downstream
```

## Rules

- **Store only the converted model.** The DTO exists to deserialize; convert and discard. Never keep the raw DTO list alongside the typed model — two rosters drift.
- **No parallel per-entity collections.** State keyed by the same id as an entity list belongs *on the entity* (`IsReady`, `CardCount` fields), not in a side `HashSet`/`Dictionary` that must be manually kept "in lockstep".
- **Derived data has one recompute point.** Values computed from the collection (disambiguated display names, sort keys, aggregates) are rewritten by exactly one helper called at the end of every mutation — never patched piecemeal at individual call sites.
- **Fallbacks are boundary decisions.** Invalid/missing input is logged and defaulted where it enters; consumers never carry their own fallback.
- **Changed signatures carry the type.** If a method needs a `UnitKind`, its parameter is `UnitKind` — not a string the callee validates.

## Allowed exceptions

- DTO types themselves at the (de)serialization boundary — they are the wire format and stay string-based.
- Code playing the *server/storage* role (local sim servers, PlayerPrefs persistence) — strings are its wire format.
- Caches explicitly serving entities that no longer exist (departed-player name cache for a game-over screen). Document them as caches; never read them for live entities.
- Logging/diagnostics of the raw value at the boundary.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Every `TryParse`/`Enum.Parse` of external data sits at an ingestion point, not in a consumer
- [ ] No raw DTO stored alongside its typed model
- [ ] No new `HashSet`/`Dictionary` keyed by entity id next to an entity list — fold into the entity
- [ ] Derived data recomputed by one helper on mutation, never patched at call sites
- [ ] Fallback-on-invalid decided (and logged) once at the boundary
